#N canvas 40 40 900 560 12;
#X obj 30 90 notein;
#X obj 30 140 pack f f f;
#X obj 30 180 print RX-NOTE;
#X obj 30 240 ctlin;
#X obj 30 290 pack f f f;
#X obj 30 330 print RX-CC;
#X obj 470 90 netreceive 9996 1;
#X obj 470 140 route prog live show clear;
#X msg 470 200 240 \, 0 \, 32 \, 41 \, 2 \, 14 \, 14 \, 1 \, 247;
#X msg 640 200 240 \, 0 \, 32 \, 41 \, 2 \, 14 \, 14 \, 0 \, 247;
#X obj 470 270 midiout;
#X text 30 20 LAUNCHPAD CC PROBE -- built for the one ring button never pressed \, CC 99 at the top-right corner \, which ref-midi marks ⬜ with the note that nothing needs it. Reads notes AND CC with their channel. Run on the MAC with -midiindev N -midioutdev N pointing at the LPProMK3 MIDI port \, which makes the Launchpad Pd's first opened slot -- so it arrives on channel 1 and midiout with no argument reaches it., f 100;
#X text 470 320 ⚠️ THE LIVE MESSAGE IS NOT OPTIONAL. Entering Programmer Mode by SysEx LOCKS OUT the device's own Settings menu \, and nothing but the Live SysEx brings it back -- a killed Pd leaves the grid frozen and the front panel unable to recover it. Send live BEFORE quitting. tools/lp-live.sh is the rescue if you forget \, and it needs no Pd at all., f 56;
#X text 30 400 DRIVEN OVER UDP 9996 \, not by clicking \, for the same reason the benches stopped driving themselves: the console text and the physical surface must not move at the same instant. echo "prog\;" or "live\;" as a datagram., f 60;
#X msg 300 470 240 \, 0 \, 32 \, 41 \, 2 \, 14 \, 3 \, 0 \, 94 \, 3 \, 1 \, 99 \, 5 \, 3 \, 247;
#X msg 300 510 240 \, 0 \, 32 \, 41 \, 2 \, 14 \, 3 \, 0 \, 94 \, 0 \, 0 \, 99 \, 0 \, 247;
#X text 300 545 SHOW lights CC 94 STATIC WHITE as a landmark -- a button known to exist because it was just pressed -- and CC 99 FLASHING. A flashing LED among static ones is unmistakable \, which is the point: it settles whether the operator and the documentation mean the SAME PHYSICAL OBJECT before any null result is trusted., f 60;
